This dissertation focuses on UAV-based remote sensing for cotton maturity monitoring in mechanized harvesting regions of Xinjiang. The study targets two key agronomic indicators, defoliation rate and boll opening rate, and develops an integrated pipeline that covers feature modeling, lightweight recognition, data balancing, and system-level validation.

First, a multi-temporal dataset is built from RGB and multispectral UAV imagery. Color, texture, and structural features are fused to establish interpretable regression baselines for both indicators. Second, to meet edge deployment constraints, a lightweight deep learning model is designed for real-time dual-indicator estimation. Knowledge distillation and quantization-aware strategies are introduced to improve the balance between accuracy and latency.

Third, considering severe illumination imbalance in field data, a conditional diffusion-based augmentation framework is proposed to synthesize samples under underrepresented lighting conditions. A quality-gating mechanism is further applied to select reliable generated samples, which improves robustness in low-light and complex illumination scenarios. Based on the balanced dataset, an all-weather recognition model with illumination-adaptive design is developed and evaluated across multiple time periods and weather conditions.

Finally, the recognition outputs are integrated with field geo-grids to generate UAV spraying prescription maps, and a simulation experiment is conducted to verify engineering feasibility in practical operation workflows. Experimental results indicate that the proposed method can deliver stable estimation of maturity indicators while maintaining real-time performance, and can effectively support variable-rate defoliant application decisions.

This work provides a deployable technical solution for intelligent cotton maturity monitoring and precision operation management, and offers a practical reference for robust visual perception in agricultural environments with dynamic illumination.
